draising, or market pricing reported, keeping uncertainty wide"], "rationale": "Rhode Island's eight-point Democratic lean, reinforced by the absence of a Republican incumbent on the ballot and Democratic momentum in 2026 special elections, places the state clearly but not overwhelmingly in the Democratic column; with no polls or ratings available, the win probability stops short of safe.", "watch": "The first 2028 Rhode Island horse-race polls, expert ratings, or a sharp shift in the national environment would be the most likely triggers for revision.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island enters the 2028 presidential race leaning Democratic, with a partisan advantage of roughly eight points and an open contest after the term-limited Republican president leaves office. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's partisan lean and the national climate.\n\nRecent signs favor Democrats, who won special elections in Republican-leaning territory through 2026, though Rhode Island also saw an incumbent Democratic governor lose a primary amid voter frustration. Republicans would need an unusually favorable national environment to put the state in play. The state's presidential primary moves to March in 2028, and the Democratic field remains unsettled after Gina Raimondo declined to run. The race is expected to stay Democratic unless the national picture shifts sharply.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8249, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7734, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7988, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7688, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8114, "margin